WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.280
Rockets shooting from where? Watching&nbsp;
your dad's head get lopped off by a Jedi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.240
is bound to mess you up, but how does the new&nbsp;
ruler of Jabba's Palace define himself today?

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.720
Even by the standards of other&nbsp;
bounty hunters in Mandalorian armor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.440
Boba Fett has a special relationship with deadly&nbsp;
gadgets. Fett's jetpack comes complete with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.680
missile that can take down entire ships.&nbsp;
His armor comes equipped with special knee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:28.400
rockets he can fire while walking toward his&nbsp;
targets. In several non-canonical stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.000
it's established that Boba Fett's&nbsp;
signature helmet, aside from looking cool,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:37.200
also gives him 360-degree vision. While this&nbsp;
didn't seem to help him avoid Han Solo's blind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.160
attack in "Return of the Jedi," this feature may&nbsp;
make a return in Fett's new canonical stories.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.960
Considering how Boba Fett's armor makes him&nbsp;
a one-man army, it's no wonder that Marshal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:52.080
Cobb Vanth was reluctant to part with the&nbsp;
armor in the season 2 "Mandalorian" episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.720
"The Marshal." Even among Mandalorians, Boba&nbsp;
Fett's tools make him a force to be reckoned with.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.720
The "Star Wars" universe is a rough&nbsp;
place where you have to fight dirty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:05.440
but sometimes Boba Fett makes fighting dirty&nbsp;
downright filthy. Initially, this was only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.680
hinted at in "The Empire Strikes Back" when&nbsp;
Darth Vader singles out Fett by telling him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.480
that there will be "no disintegrations."&nbsp;
In the canon comic book story "Star Wars:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:20.800
Age of Republic —Jango Fett" #1, a 10-year-old&nbsp;
Boba executes two experienced bounty hunters when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:25.440
they try to take him hostage, proving he had&nbsp;
the ability to be brutal even at a young age.

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:30.560
But it's in "The Mandalorian" season 2 episode&nbsp;
"The Tragedy" that we learn just how merciless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:36.640
Boba Fett is with his targets. Stripped of his&nbsp;
armor, Boba uses a Tusken Raider gaderffii stick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:42.320
against a squad of Stormtroopers who probably&nbsp;
all wish he would just disintegrate them. Fett&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:46.240
caves in their skulls and brutally&nbsp;
impales them with his melee weapon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:50.160
giving them some of the most painful-looking&nbsp;
deaths in "Star Wars" history. Later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:55.040
he recovers his armor and takes out the remaining&nbsp;
Stormtroopers with bombs, blaster shots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:00.240
knee rockets, and a very well-placed jetpack&nbsp;
missile. By the end of this scene, everyone knows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:06.080
that when Boba Fett has you in his sights, you can&nbsp;
forget about running away. You're already dead.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.600
"Star Wars" characters face adversity all&nbsp;
the time, but few of them face it as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.840
as Boba Fett, whose entire life seems to&nbsp;
be fighting with his back against the wall.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.480
While Boba receives training and guidance from his&nbsp;
father Jango for the first few years of his life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.400
he essentially needs to make it on his own&nbsp;
from the age of 10 after he's orphaned in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:29.680
the Battle of Geonosis. He chooses to make it&nbsp;
in the cutthroat world of bounty hunting. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:34.160
at the height of his reputation, he falls&nbsp;
into the Sarlacc pit in "Return of the Jedi"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.160
and must keep himself from being slowly&nbsp;
digested over the next thousand years.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:44.000
Yet somehow Fett survives all of these impossible&nbsp;
odds, adding to the aura of danger and mystique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.640
around him. When we see him in the end credits&nbsp;
scene of The Mandalorian episode "The Rescue,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:53.520
he's seemingly acquired a position of great&nbsp;
power as the new crime boss of Tatooine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:56.400
but it's basically a given that&nbsp;
this will require him to perform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.600
even more impossible feats when&nbsp;
"The Book of Boba Fett" debuts.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.000
Boba Fett may have initially shown&nbsp;
up in the service of the Empire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.480
but he quickly establishes that he's really&nbsp;
just following the money, and in it for himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.360
Shortly after tracking Han Solo to Bespin&nbsp;
for Darth Vader in "The Empire Strikes Back,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:18.000
Fett collects the bounty on Captain Solo&nbsp;
by turning in his carbonite-frozen form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.960
to the Tatooine crime lord Jabba the Hutt in&nbsp;
"Return of the Jedi." He remains in Jabba's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.920
employ until he falls in the Sarlacc pit,&nbsp;
and then roams the galaxy alone, eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:33.280
forming a temporary alliance with Mandalorian&nbsp;
Din Djarin against the remains of the Empire.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:38.720
As Fett clearly states to Din Djarin in "The&nbsp;
Mandalorian" season 2 episode "The Tragedy,"

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:40.800
"I give my allegiance to no one."

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:46.320
Unlike Mandalorians, who follow a specific creed&nbsp;
that dictates their actions, Boba Fett makes his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:51.040
own rules. This might make him unpopular, but for&nbsp;
this bounty hunter, it's the only way to live.

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.720
Of course, just because Boba Fett lives by&nbsp;
his own rules doesn't mean he's an entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:01.600
lawless man. He may be brutal, but he will honor&nbsp;
the terms of any deals he makes with others.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:06.960
Sometimes those deals are formal contracts he&nbsp;
makes with regimes like the Empire or powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:12.160
men like Darth Vader. Other times, they're verbal&nbsp;
agreements he offers to bounty hunters like Din&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:17.280
Djarin, which places him in the Mandalorian's&nbsp;
debt until he helps him save the Child known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:22.800
Grogu. Either way, Boba Fett shows he will stand&nbsp;
by deals made with his employers and allies.

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:28.480
That said, once the terms of the deal are complete&nbsp;
or his employers try to stab him in the back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:33.040
all bets are off. Fett may have been working&nbsp;
for Darth Vader in "The Empire Strikes Back,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:37.840
but he has no problem killing Stormtroopers or&nbsp;
blowing up TIE Fighters in "The Mandalorian"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:42.160
once he enters into a new deal with Din&nbsp;
Djarin. He may be a dangerous mercenary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:45.520
but as long as he's working for&nbsp;
you, Boba Fett will go the distance.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:51.600
Until Boba Fett acquires a partner in Fennec Shand&nbsp;
after he saves her following the events of The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:57.760
Mandalorian season 1 episode "The Gunslinger," he&nbsp;
usually travels alone. Nevertheless, this bounty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:02.640
hunter is far from a man without a past or a&nbsp;
family. While he doesn't necessarily identify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.640 --> 00:05:08.320
as a Mandalorian, Fett is proud of his family&nbsp;
line and identifies strongly as Jango Fett's son.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:12.400
This causes him to respond with extreme&nbsp;
anger if someone tries to write him off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:17.520
as just another Clone Trooper. He gets into a&nbsp;
violent brawl with Koska Reeves after she and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.240
Bo-Katan sneer at his clone origins in&nbsp;
"The Mandalorian" episode "The Rescue."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:26.400
He also takes great pride in showing Din&nbsp;
Djarin the record of his family tree stored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.640
in his armor, which just provides him with an&nbsp;
additional reason to reclaim his famous suit.

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:36.480
If there's one thing that distinguishes Boba Fett,&nbsp;
it's his trademark Mandalorian armor. While there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:41.200
are now multiple Mandalorian bounty hunters and&nbsp;
warriors running around the "Star Wars" universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:45.920
there's just something about Fett's battle-scarred&nbsp;
green and gray armor that tells people they're in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:50.880
the presence of a dangerous man with a very&nbsp;
personal connection to his chosen wardrobe.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:55.120
"I don't want your armor. I want my armor."

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:59.360
In recent episodes of "The Mandalorian,"&nbsp;
Fett has cleaned up the scratches and blaster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:03.840
marks from his armor, and re-established the&nbsp;
important reasons for wearing this particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:08.720
suit. While Boba Fett clearly has the means&nbsp;
to have a new armored suit built for him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:11.200
he insists on tracking down his original armor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:16.080
The suit initially belonged to Jango Fett, after&nbsp;
all, and is his strongest link to his father.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:20.640
For someone who's lost as much as Boba&nbsp;
Fett, his armor truly is irreplaceable.

